(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xii"] = {"qs": [["Primary Markets", "In a fixed price issue, investors know:", ["Only a price band before applying", "The issue price before applying", "The price only after listing", "Nothing about the price until allotment"], 1, "In a fixed price issue the price is stated in the offer document before bidding. In a book-built issue only a price band is known and the final price is set after bidding."], ["Secondary Markets", "A price band on an individual stock limits:", ["How far its price can move in a day", "The number of shares that can be bought", "The brokerage that can be charged", "The dividend the company can pay"], 0, "Price bands or circuit filters restrict the daily price range of a stock to curb excessive volatility. They do not limit quantities, brokerage or dividends."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "Which security typically offers the highest potential return along with the highest risk?", ["Government treasury bills", "Bank fixed deposits", "Equity shares", "AAA-rated corporate bonds"], 2, "Equity holders are residual claimants, so returns are uncertain but potentially high. Treasury bills, deposits and top-rated bonds pay fixed amounts with lower risk."], ["Primary Markets", "Which investors can apply in the anchor investor portion of an IPO?", ["Qualified institutional buyers", "Retail individual investors", "Employees of the issuer only", "Non-resident individuals only"], 0, "Anchor investors are QIBs who apply for a large minimum amount a day before the issue opens, giving price confidence. Retail investors bid in their own category."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Price discovery in the secondary market happens through:", ["A price set by SEBI every morning", "The issuer announcing a daily price", "Interaction of buy and sell orders", "The depository fixing a price"], 2, "Prices emerge from the matching of many buy and sell orders on the exchange, reflecting available information and demand and supply. No authority fixes daily prices."], ["Secondary Markets", "Pay-out in the settlement process means:", ["Investors pay money to the exchange", "Brokers collect margins from clients", "The exchange cancels unsettled trades", "Buyers get securities and sellers get funds"], 3, "Pay-out is when the clearing corporation delivers securities to buyers and funds to sellers. Pay-in is when buyers pay funds and sellers deliver securities."], ["Derivative Markets", "Why do derivatives carry high risk for small investors?", ["They are not regulated by SEBI", "Their prices never change", "They cannot be sold before expiry", "Leverage magnifies losses"], 3, "Derivatives require only margin for large exposure, so small price moves cause large losses relative to capital. They are regulated and can be closed before expiry."], ["Mutual Funds", "Which statement about mutual fund returns is correct?", ["They are not guaranteed", "They are guaranteed by SEBI", "They are fixed at the time of NFO", "They are insured by DICGC"], 0, "Mutual fund returns depend on the performance of underlying investments and are not guaranteed. DICGC insurance covers bank deposits, not fund units."], ["Secondary Markets", "Which service lets investors file and track complaints against listed companies and intermediaries online?", ["RBI Retail Direct", "NSDL e-voting", "SEBI SCORES", "AMFI's NAV page"], 2, "SCORES is SEBI's online complaint redress system, linked to market-wide dispute resolution. Retail Direct is for G-secs, e-voting for shareholder resolutions and AMFI's page for NAVs."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "A depository participant (DP) acts as:", ["The regulator of stock exchanges", "The issuer of new shares", "The investor's link to the depository", "A bank that lends to listed companies"], 2, "DPs, such as banks and brokers, open demat accounts and offer depository services as agents of NSDL or CDSL. SEBI is the regulator and companies are issuers."], ["Secondary Markets", "The role of the clearing corporation in the secondary market is to:", ["List new companies on the exchange", "Hold investors' securities in demat form", "Fix brokerage rates for all brokers", "Guarantee settlement of trades"], 3, "The clearing corporation clears and settles trades and guarantees settlement through novation, removing counterparty risk. Depositories hold securities."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "An investor with a 3-year goal and low risk appetite should give most weight to:", ["Debt instruments", "Small-cap shares", "Equity derivatives", "Sector funds"], 0, "Short horizons and low risk tolerance call for stable instruments such as deposits and debt funds. Small caps, derivatives and sector funds are volatile."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "A company has a P/E ratio of 25 and EPS of ₹16. What is its share price?", ["₹41", "₹1.56", "₹250", "₹400"], 3, "Price = P/E × EPS = 25 × 16 = ₹400. The other options add the numbers or divide them, which misapplies the definition of the P/E ratio."], ["Primary Markets", "Who decides the final issue price in a book-built issue?", ["SEBI", "The stock exchange", "The retail investors' association", "The issuer with the lead managers"], 3, "After bids close, the issuer and book running lead managers determine the final price within the band based on demand. SEBI does not set prices."], ["Derivative Markets", "A company that will import goods in three months buys currency futures to fix its rupee cost. It is:", ["Speculating", "Hedging", "Arbitraging", "Market making"], 1, "Using derivatives to offset an existing business exposure is hedging. Speculation takes positions without an underlying exposure."], ["Derivative Markets", "A rise in open interest along with rising prices usually suggests:", ["Short sellers closing positions", "No trading activity", "New long positions being added", "The contract has expired"], 2, "Rising open interest with rising prices typically indicates fresh buying. Short covering usually reduces open interest while prices rise."], ["Secondary Markets", "An investor buys 50 shares at ₹400 and sells them at ₹380. Ignoring charges, his loss is:", ["₹20", "₹2,000", "₹1,000", "₹500"], 2, "Loss = (400 − 380) × 50 = ₹1,000. ₹20 is the loss per share, not the total."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Which is an example of a financial security?", ["A corporate bond", "A residential flat", "A tonne of wheat", "A painting"], 0, "Securities are tradable financial instruments such as shares, bonds and units. Flats, commodities and art are physical assets, not securities."], ["Derivative Markets", "An investor buys one futures lot of 500 shares at ₹300 and sells it at ₹310. Ignoring costs, her profit is:", ["₹5,000", "₹10", "₹1,500", "₹50,000"], 0, "Profit = (310 − 300) × 500 = ₹5,000. ₹10 is the profit per share; multiplying by lot size gives the total."], ["Mutual Funds", "A balanced advantage fund mainly aims to:", ["Hold only government bonds", "Invest only in gold", "Track a single sector index", "Shift between equity and debt dynamically"], 3, "Balanced advantage (dynamic asset allocation) funds change their equity and debt mix based on market conditions or valuation models."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "Technical analysis mainly studies:", ["A company's balance sheet", "Past prices and volumes", "Management quality", "Industry growth prospects"], 1, "Technical analysts study price and volume charts to identify trends and patterns. Fundamental analysts examine financial statements, management and industry prospects."], ["Secondary Markets", "An investor places a market order to buy 100 shares. This order will:", ["Execute only at a price he specifies", "Execute at the best available price", "Remain pending until the next day", "Be cancelled if the price moves"], 1, "A market order executes immediately at the best price available in the order book. A limit order executes only at the specified price or better."], ["Primary Markets", "A company allots shares to a select group of investors, such as a strategic partner, on a non-public basis. This is a:", ["Rights issue", "Bonus issue", "Initial public offer", "Preferential allotment"], 3, "A preferential allotment issues shares to specific persons, subject to ICDR pricing and lock-in rules. A rights issue goes to all existing holders pro rata."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "How do securities markets help the economy grow?", ["By moving savings to productive businesses", "By fixing the prices of all goods and services", "By printing money for companies", "By guaranteeing returns to all savers"], 0, "Securities markets let companies and governments raise funds from savers for productive investment. They do not fix goods prices, print money or guarantee returns."], ["Secondary Markets", "A stop-loss sell order is typically used by an investor who:", ["Wants to buy only when prices fall", "Wants to limit losses if prices fall", "Wants to ensure the highest selling price", "Wants to avoid any execution at all"], 1, "A stop-loss sell order becomes active when the price falls to a trigger, helping cap losses on a holding. It does not guarantee the best price."], ["Derivative Markets", "The maximum loss for an option buyer is:", ["Unlimited", "The premium paid", "The strike price", "Twice the premium"], 1, "An option buyer can let the option lapse, so the most he can lose is the premium. Option sellers face larger potential losses."], ["Derivative Markets", "A put option buyer profits when the underlying price:", ["Rises above the strike price", "Stays exactly at the strike", "Rises above strike plus premium", "Falls below strike minus premium"], 3, "A put gains when the underlying falls; net profit starts below strike minus premium. Rising prices hurt a put buyer."], ["Secondary Markets", "Why should an investor check the contract note received from his broker?", ["To verify trade details and charges", "To find the next day's market trend", "To claim dividends from the company", "To change his demat account number"], 0, "The contract note confirms price, quantity, time and charges for each trade, helping investors detect errors or unauthorised trades quickly."], ["Primary Markets", "A bonus issue means that a company:", ["Sells new shares to the public at a discount", "Buys back shares from shareholders", "Gives free shares to existing holders", "Issues debentures to banks"], 2, "A bonus issue capitalises reserves into free shares for existing shareholders. It raises no fresh money and does not change each holder's proportionate stake."], ["Mutual Funds", "Who appoints the asset management company of a mutual fund and oversees it on behalf of unitholders?", ["The trustees", "The custodian", "The registrar", "The distributor"], 0, "Trustees protect unitholders' interests, appoint the AMC with SEBI approval and monitor its compliance. Custodians hold assets and registrars maintain records."], ["Mutual Funds", "An investor invests ₹10,000 in a scheme at a NAV of ₹25. How many units does she get, ignoring charges?", ["250 units", "400 units", "2,500 units", "40 units"], 1, "Units = amount ÷ NAV = 10,000 ÷ 25 = 400. The other options misplace the decimal or divide the wrong way."], ["Primary Markets", "What does the term 'oversubscribed' mean for an issue?", ["Bids exceed the shares on offer", "Fewer bids than shares on offer", "The issue was cancelled by SEBI", "Shares listed below the issue price"], 0, "An issue is oversubscribed when demand exceeds supply; allotment is then made as per the basis of allotment, with retail investors often receiving the minimum lot by lottery."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "An investor buys a share at ₹200 and sells it after a year at ₹230, receiving no dividend. What is her return?", ["15%", "30%", "13%", "23%"], 0, "Return = (230 − 200) ÷ 200 = 15%. 13% wrongly divides the gain by the selling price, and 30% is the rupee gain, not a percentage."], ["Derivative Markets", "A trader buys a call with a strike of ₹200 for a premium of ₹8. At expiry the stock is at ₹215. What is the net profit per share?", ["₹15", "₹23", "₹7", "₹8"], 2, "Payoff = 215 − 200 = ₹15; net profit = 15 − 8 = ₹7. ₹15 ignores the premium paid."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "₹1 lakh invested at 10% compounded annually becomes how much after 3 years?", ["₹1,30,000", "₹1,10,000", "₹1,33,100", "₹1,33,000"], 2, "FV = 1,00,000 × 1.1³ = ₹1,33,100. ₹1,30,000 is the simple-interest value, which ignores interest earned on interest."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "Why does inflation matter to investors?", ["It increases the face value of bonds", "It reduces the real value of returns", "It guarantees higher share prices", "It has no effect on savings"], 1, "Inflation erodes purchasing power, so returns must be judged after inflation. A deposit earning less than inflation loses real value."], ["Mutual Funds", "Which fund type aims to match an index's return rather than beat it?", ["Small-cap fund", "Index fund", "Focused fund", "Value fund"], 1, "Index funds are passively managed and replicate an index, aiming for returns close to it at low cost. Active funds try to outperform their benchmarks."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Investors in the securities market include:", ["Only banks and insurance companies", "Only the central government", "Only listed companies", "Individuals, institutions and foreign investors"], 3, "Investors range from retail individuals and HUFs to mutual funds, insurers, pension funds and foreign portfolio investors. Diversity of participants adds depth to the market."], ["Mutual Funds", "A closed-ended scheme:", ["Allows purchases and redemptions daily with the AMC", "Has no maturity date at all", "Has a fixed maturity and lists units", "Can be bought only by companies"], 2, "Closed-ended schemes accept money only during the NFO, have a fixed term and list units on exchanges for liquidity. Open-ended schemes allow continuous transactions."], ["Primary Markets", "Before investing in an IPO, an investor should read the:", ["Company's advertisements only", "Offer document's risk factors", "Social media posts on the IPO", "Grey market premium updates"], 1, "The offer document gives detailed information including risk factors, financials and objects of the issue. Advertisements and social media are not a substitute."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Liquidity in a securities market means that investors can:", ["Get guaranteed profits on every trade", "Buy or sell quickly at fair prices", "Hold securities only in physical form", "Avoid paying any transaction costs"], 1, "A liquid market has many buyers and sellers, so trades can be executed quickly without large price impact. Liquidity does not guarantee profits or remove costs."], ["Primary Markets", "Which intermediary handles applications, allotment and refunds in a public issue?", ["Credit rating agency", "Custodian", "Registrar to the issue", "Stock broker"], 2, "The registrar processes applications, finalises allotment with the exchange and coordinates unblocking or refunds. Rating agencies, custodians and brokers have other roles."], ["Securities, Asset Allocation and Investing", "A bond's coupon rate is:", ["The price at which it trades", "Its yield to maturity", "The rating given by an agency", "The interest rate on its face value"], 3, "The coupon rate is the annual interest paid as a percentage of face value. YTM depends on the market price, and ratings measure credit quality."], ["Mutual Funds", "A scheme's NAV fell from ₹40 to ₹36 over a year with no payouts. The return is:", ["−4%", "−11.1%", "−10%", "+10%"], 2, "Return = (36 − 40) ÷ 40 = −10%. −11.1% divides by the ending NAV, and −4% is the rupee change per unit."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Which regulator oversees stock brokers and stock exchanges in India?", ["RBI", "SEBI", "IRDAI", "PFRDA"], 1, "SEBI regulates stock exchanges, brokers, depositories and other securities market intermediaries. RBI regulates banks and money markets, IRDAI insurance and PFRDA pensions."], ["Mutual Funds", "An exchange traded fund differs from a regular open-ended index fund because ETF units:", ["Are bought only from the AMC at day-end NAV", "Carry guaranteed returns", "Have no expense ratio at all", "Trade on a stock exchange during market hours"], 3, "ETF units are listed and traded on exchanges at market prices throughout the day. Open-ended index fund units are bought and redeemed with the AMC at NAV."], ["Securities Markets and Their Role", "Which of the following is a market infrastructure institution in the Indian securities market?", ["A mutual fund distributor", "An investment adviser", "A listed company", "A stock exchange"], 3, "Stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories are market infrastructure institutions that provide core trading, clearing and holding systems. Distributors, advisers and issuers use this infrastructure."], ["Derivative Markets", "Index futures in India are settled at expiry by:", ["Cash settlement", "Physical delivery of all index stocks", "Delivery of an ETF", "Rolling over automatically"], 0, "An index cannot be delivered, so index futures and options are cash settled based on the final settlement price."]]};
